  "name": "Question 25",
  "problem": "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ then $D({e}^{x})=$",
  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=y,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={e}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{x})-x({e}^{x})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(1-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
